This package contains no source code from other software.  Its algorithms are
implemented from the published literature; each source file names the papers it
follows, and docs/src/theory.md lists them in full.  The principal ones are:

* Band-limited shearlets: S. Häuser and G. Steidl, "Fast Finite Shearlet
  Transform: a tutorial", arXiv:1202.1773, 2012; K. Guo and D. Labate, Math.
  Model. Nat. Phenom. 8(1), 2013; F. Voigtlaender and A. Pein,
  arXiv:1702.03559, 2017.
* 3-D band-limited shearlets: P. S. Negi and D. Labate, IEEE Trans. Image
  Process. 21(6), 2012.
* Nonsubsampled shearlet transform: G. Easley, D. Labate and W.-Q. Lim, Appl.
  Comput. Harmon. Anal. 25(1), 2008.
* Compactly supported shearlets: G. Kutyniok, W.-Q. Lim and R. Reisenhofer,
  "ShearLab 3D", ACM Trans. Math. Softw. 42(1), 2016; W.-Q. Lim, IEEE Trans.
  Image Process. 22(5), 2013.
* Maximally flat fan filter: A. L. da Cunha, J. Zhou and M. N. Do, IEEE Trans.
  Image Process. 15(10), 2006.

The default quadrature mirror filter of the compactly supported shearlets is the
9-tap maximally flat lowpass that ShearLab 3D uses; its taps are the numbers
MATLAB's maxflat filter design returns for that specification.

pyshearlab (GNU General Public License) is neither included in nor derived into
this package.  It was used only as a black-box reference program, run to compare
outputs; no test data in this repository comes from it.  MATLAB's
shearletSystem (Wavelet Toolbox) is mentioned for comparison only.
